        "input": "4\n3\n-1 -1 2\n1 2\n2 3\n3\n5 -10 2\n1 2\n2 3\n2\n1000000000 1000000000\n1 2\n6\n1 -1 1 -1 1 -1\n2 4\n4 6\n2 1\n4 3\n6 5",
        "output": "16\n32\n319630959\n16",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: One optimal walk is (1,2,3)(1, 2, 3)(1,2,3), which starts at 111 and ends at 333, moving through 222.\nThe score of this walk is as follows:\n\nStart with P=1P = 1P=1.\nFirst vertex is 111. A1=−1<0A_1 = -1 \\lt 0A1​=−1<0, so we multiply −2−A1=−2-2^{-A_1} = -2−2−A1​=−2 to PPP.\nNow, P=−2P = -2P=−2.\nSecond vertex is 222. A2=−1<0A_2 = -1 \\lt 0A2​=−1<0, so we multiply −2−A2=−2-2^{-A_2} = -2−2−A2​=−2 to PPP.\nNow, P=4P = 4P=4.\nThird vertex is 333. A3=2>0A_3 = 2 \\gt 0A3​=2>0, so we multiply 2A3=22=42^{A_3} = 2^2 = 42A3​=22=4 to PPP.\nNow, P=16P = 16P=16.\n\nIt can be proved that this is optimal.\nTest case 222: One optimal walk is (1)(1)(1), i.e. to start at 111 and just not move.\nSince A1>0A_1 \\gt 0A1​>0, the score of this walk is 2A1=25=322^{A_1} = 2^5 = 322A1​=25=32."
